1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:05,600
A candy bar melted in an engineer's pocket — and whatever melted it was invisible.

2
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:07,915
It was the mid-nineteen-forties.

3
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:14,167
Percy Spencer had been standing beside a magnetron — the tube that sends out radar's invisible waves.

4
00:00:14,567 --> 00:00:18,017
Instead of shrugging it off, he tested food on purpose.

5
00:00:18,117 --> 00:00:20,526
Popcorn kernels by the magnetron popped.

6
00:00:20,626 --> 00:00:22,000
An egg exploded.

7
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:26,103
Then the key step: he concentrated the waves in a small space.

8
00:00:26,203 --> 00:00:28,200
Food placed there heated fast.

9
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:34,167
In October, nineteen forty-five, Raytheon filed a patent for cooking with microwaves.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,734
Microwave ovens still work that way — a magnetron filling a metal box with waves.

11
00:00:39,834 --> 00:00:45,833
Every time you press Start, you're using the answer to the question a melted candy bar raised.
